<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meuse</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Maas, River Meuse</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Meuse (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>: Maas) is a river of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, about 925 km long, which rises on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langres">Langres</a> plateau in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and flows through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. Since Roman times it has been both a trade route and a frontier, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> of 843 used it to divide the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Frankish kingdoms</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a>, Sedan, Namur, Liège and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht">Maastricht</a> lie on its banks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meuse.jpg" alt="Meuse" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marc Ryckaert · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dinant_Meuse_R01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Meuse rises at Pouilly-en-Bassigny, in the commune of Le Châtelet-sur-Meuse on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langres">Langres</a> plateau, and flows north past Sedan, the head of navigation, and Charleville-Mézières into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. At Namur it is joined by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambre">Sambre</a>. Beyond Namur it winds east, passes Liège and turns north, forming part of the Belgian-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> border. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> it passes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venlo">Venlo</a> and turns west to join the delta it shares with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a>.</p><p>The name comes from the Latin Mosa, which derives from a Celtic or Proto-Celtic name probably related to the English word maze, a reference to the river's twists and turns.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From 1301 the upper Meuse roughly marked the western border of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> with the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, after Count Henry III of Bar had to receive the western part of the County of Bar as a French fief from King Philip IV. In 1408 a Burgundian army led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-fearless">John the Fearless</a> helped John III against the citizens of Liège, who were in revolt. After defeating them, John ordered the drowning in the Meuse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> and nobles whose loyalties he suspected.</p><p>The border stayed relatively stable until King Henry II annexed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-bishoprics">Three Bishoprics</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toul">Toul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> in 1552, and until the occupation of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> by the forces of King Louis XIII in 1633. The lower Belgian, Walloon, stretch of the valley, part of the sillon industriel, was the first fully industrialised area on the European continent.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, a flood in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> connected the Maas with the Merwede at Woudrichem. In the late 19th and early 20th centuries the connection with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> was closed off and the Maas was given a new artificial mouth, the Bergse Maas, which reduced the risk of flooding. This was regarded as the main achievement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> hydraulic engineering before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuiderzee-works">Zuiderzee Works</a> and Delta Works. The Meuse and its crossings were a key objective in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-france">Battle of France</a> and the Battle of Sedan, and again in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-bulge">Battle of the Bulge</a> in December 1944 and January 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The river is about 925 km long and is navigable over a substantial part of its length, linking the ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rotterdam">Rotterdam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> with industrial areas upstream as far as Liège and Namur. The river's basin of about 36,000 km2 lies mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wallonia">Wallonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. As a major communication route the Meuse gave its name to Mosan art, and Joachim Patinir painted its landscape.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Meuse” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Trade existed but was constrained by poor infrastructure: rivers such as the Rhine, <b>Meuse</b> and Seine were the main commercial arteries, linking inland markets, episcopal cities and monastic fairs to North Sea trading centres such as Dorestad and Quentovic, where Frisian merchants dominated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Diet of Schwäbisch Hall in 1190 Emperor Henry VI confirmed that the ducal rank applied to Henry's own territories, which included Louvain, Brussels, Antwerp and lands stretching north towards the <b>Meuse</b>, rather than to the whole of Lower Lorraine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 959 Archbishop Bruno of Cologne, brother of Otto I and duke of Lotharingia, divided it into Lower Lorraine in the north and Upper Lorraine along the Moselle and upper <b>Meuse</b>, entrusting the south to Frederick, count of Bar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In Toxandria, the Salian Franks farmed land between the <b>Meuse</b> and the Scheldt under Roman authority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/franks">Franks · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Liège lay on the <b>Meuse</b> in the old Carolingian heartland, and after Lotharingia joined the German kingdom its bishops became pillars of the Ottonian imperial church, whose emperors relied on loyal prelates rather than hereditary dukes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Prince-Bishopric of Liège · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Zwentibold, king of Lotharingia since 895, was killed in battle on the <b>Meuse</b> near present-day Susteren by nobles who had risen against him under Reginar Longneck.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-13">13 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
